  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The District of Columbia received $26B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Contracting in the District included 38,737 awards, with an average award size of $670K. Obligations fell 17.4% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a peak of $36B in FY24, with FY21 at $31B, FY22 at $31.6B, FY23 at $33.9B, FY25 at $31.4B, and FY26 at $26B. Computer Systems Design Services led industries with $3.8B in obligations, followed by Engineering Services with $3.2B and Other Computer Related Services with $3.1B. The Department of Defense accounted for $5B in obligations, the Department of Transportation accounted for $3.5B, and the General Services Administration accounted for $3.4B. BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC was the largest vendor with $986M, followed by DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP with $890M and HARRIS CORPORATION with $780M. All figures reported are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in the District trended recently?",
        "body": "FY26 obligations stood at $26B, down from $31.4B in FY25 and down 17.4% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows FY21 at $31B, FY22 at $31.6B, FY23 at $33.9B, and FY24 at $36B. Current-year totals are partial because FY2026 is fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and contractors dominate District obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $5B, the Department of Transportation obligated $3.5B, and the General Services Administration obligated $3.4B. BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC received $986M, DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP received $890M, and HARRIS CORPORATION received $780M. Computer Systems Design Services was the largest industry with $3.8B, followed by Engineering Services with $3.2B."
      }
    ]
  },
